---
import Marketing from '~/layouts/Marketing.astro';
import { SITE, REPO_URL, issue } from '~/lib/site';
const n = SITE.name;
const mail = SITE.contactEmail
  ? `mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(`${n} waitlist`)}`
  : null;
---

<Marketing title="Join the waitlist" description={`Get told when ${n} installs for anyone, not just its builder.`}>
  <header class="m-pagehead m-narrow">
    <p class="m-eyebrow">Waitlist</p>
    <h1>{n} isn't ready to install yet. Here's how to hear first.</h1>
    <p>
      It runs every day on my machines, but setting it up still takes knowing how I set it up. When a stranger can
      install it and be guided through the rest, it ships as the first release. Until then there's no download here.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="m-narrow">
    <div class="m-grid m-join">
      <section class="m-panel">
        <p class="m-step">Option 1</p>
        <h2>Watch releases on GitHub</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open <a href={REPO_URL}>the repo</a> (a free GitHub account is all you need).</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Watch</strong>, then <strong>Custom</strong>.</li>
          <li>Tick <strong>Releases</strong> and apply.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="m-dim">You'll get one notification when the first release ships. Nothing else.</p>
        <a class="m-btn m-btn--primary" href={REPO_URL}>Open the repo on GitHub</a>
      </section>
      {
        mail ? (
          <section class="m-panel">
            <p class="m-step">Option 2</p>
            <h2>Email me</h2>
            <p class="m-dim">
              One line is plenty. Say what you'd use it for if you like. I'll write back when it's ready, once. No
              list service, no newsletter.
            </p>
            <a class="m-btn" href={mail}>Email to join</a>
          </section>
        ) : (
          <section class="m-panel">
            <p class="m-step">Option 2</p>
            <h2>Follow the work</h2>
            <p class="m-dim">
              The <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a> shows what landed and the <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a> shows
              what's next. Nothing to sign up for.
            </p>
            <a class="m-btn" href="/roadmap/">See the roadmap</a>
          </section>
        )
      }
    </div>

    <section class="m-prose m-what">
      <h2>What has to happen first</h2>
      <p>
        The blocker is the default build: a fresh download, with no help from me, that ends at a working {n}. That work
        is tracked in the open, in <a href={issue(646)}>ticket #646</a> and the tickets around it.
      </p>
      <h2>What you'll need when it's ready</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Mac. The core and its tests also run on Linux; the desktop app is macOS only today.</li>
        <li>Claude Code, signed in.</li>
        <li>Python 3.9 to 3.12, and about 300 MB for the local embedding model.</li>
        <li>A GitHub account, for tickets and pull requests.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="m-dim">
        Want to read the code before then? It's all on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>, and the <a href="/docs/">docs</a>{' '}
        explain each feature down to its source.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Marketing>

<style>
  .m-join {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .m-join h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }
  .m-join ol {
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }
  .m-step {
    font-family: var(--m-font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--m-orange);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .m-what h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }
</style>
